  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1157 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nAll eyes are on the next progged upper level trough \ncoming across the Desert Southwest and combining with what will \nbecome tropical depression Polo late Monday night into Wednesday \nnight. This particular set-up, is expected to lead to a flash flood \npotential to the combined Panhandles. The central to western \ncombined Panhandles currently have at least a 40% probability to see \nflash flooding within 25 miles of a given point, between 7 am \nTuesday and 7 am Wednesday. Rivers and creeks will need to be \nwatched closely as well with the recent rainfall seen over the past \nfew days with more to come. NBM PoPs are currently sitting between \n80 and 95 percent during this 24 hour period. Slightly higher than \nthe previous NBM run of 70 to 85 PoPs. NBM Probs for at least 2 \ninches are sitting around 20 to 50 percent. However, there are some \noutliers that would suggest that some localized areas in the \nPanhandles could potentially see 4-8 inches with this system. \n\nDepending on when and if the low pressure system coming from the \nwest stalls out, rain along with a flooding potential could last \nwell into Thursday morning. Besides the rain, cloud cover is \nexpected to stay along with cooler H85 leading to afternoon highs in \nthe 70s to maybe lower 80s Tue through Fri. Highs in the lower to \nmid 80s may return until next Saturday.",
